            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": ">  plugins shouldn't try to restore internal state. That is just a recipe for misery.\n\nThere are some \"legit\" use cases. For instance, nvim-dap's breakpoints. I'd say it's much more \"miserable\" to not have my breakpoints restored. In fact, I'm having trouble coming up scenarios where this would be an unfortunate approach. Do you have any concrete examples where you think this could be misused?\n\nTo give a concrete example where the \"globals\" strategy is used (in what I believe to be a non-miserable situation), nvim-dap-view uses the \"globals\" [strategy](https://igorlfs.github.io/nvim-dap-view/restoring-sessions) to restore some more \"structured\" data: the full state of the watched expressions. In the grand scheme of things, that's not a big deal, but I do think it contributes as \"quality of life\" feature. This wouldn't make sense in the context of restoring from a URI (which I do agree that should be restored).\n",
