            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> There are some \"legit\" use cases. For instance, nvim-dap's breakpoints.\n\nYes, anything that can be treated as \"data\" stored in `stdpath('data')` is certainly a candidate for restoring.\n\n> Do you have any concrete examples where you think this could be misused?\n\nAny case where a plugin restores some state and forgets dependencies on other state (possibly from other plugins).\n\nPlugins are free to do whatever they want, but as general *guidance* we shouldn't set the expectation that plugins should try to maintain all of their internal state and restore it after a session. They will be full of bugs, and it makes sessions worse, not better.\n\nAs a user, I want sessions to be a reliable superset of \"start from zero\", not a roll of the dice.\n\n",
